(at your option) any later version.
See the file COPYING for more information.

Description
-----------

The plugin is a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server. It offers every SVDRP
command of VDR and of the loaded plugins as an MCP tool, so an AI assistant
like Claude can work with this VDR: list and set timers, search the EPG,
sort, check and cut recordings, look at the log. The assistant does this
on request in a chat, e.g. "sort the recordings of Suits into seasons" or
"which recordings of last week have errors?".

The tools are built at runtime from SVDRP HELP: VDR's own commands are named
vdr_<CMD> (vdr_LSTT, vdr_NEWT, ...), plugin commands <plugin>_<CMD>
(epgsearch_LSTS, satip_INFO, markad_MARK, ...). The help page of each
command is the tool description, the only argument "args" takes the command
arguments exactly as SVDRP expects them. Only the plugins that are loaded
show up. A few tools go beyond SVDRP: the system log, editing marks and the
regeneration of the index of a recording.

Two ways lead to the server:

- over SSH, for a client on a machine with SSH access to the VDR host:
  no keys, no certificates, SSH does the login;
- over HTTPS with API keys, for clients without SSH: other machines in the
  LAN, agents, automation tools. Off by default.

For several VDRs, run the plugin on each of them and add each one as its
own server in the client (vdr1, vdr2, ...).

Requirements
------------

- VDR 2.8.4 or later with SVDRP; 127.0.0.1 must be allowed in
  svdrphosts.conf (the default), as the plugin talks to the local SVDRP port
- for building: nlohmann-json, libsystemd, OpenSSL 3
- for the SSH way: socat on the VDR host
- an MCP client, e.g. Claude Code, or any other client
  that speaks MCP over stdio or "Streamable HTTP"

Quick start over SSH
--------------------

Nothing to configure on the VDR host. On the client machine:

  claude mcp add vdr -- ssh <user>@<vdr-host> socat STDIO TCP:127.0.0.1:6420

The SSH login must work without a password prompt (key based), as the
client starts ssh in the background. Every user with SSH access to the VDR
host can use the server this way.

Quick start over HTTPS
----------------------

1. Switch it on in /etc/vdr/conf.avail/mcp.conf (or wherever the plugin
   arguments are kept) and restart VDR:

     --http=0.0.0.0:6421

   At the first start the plugin creates a self-signed certificate.

2. Create a key on the VDR host:

     svdrpsend PLUG mcp KEYADD claude rw

   The reply shows the key once and the ready command line for Claude Code:

     900-API key 'claude' (rw), shown only this once:
     900-<64 hex digits>
     900-Claude Code:
     900-claude mcp add --transport http vdr https://vdr:6421/mcp --header ...
     900-Other MCP clients: URL https://vdr:6421/mcp, HTTP header ...
     900 The client must trust the certificate /var/lib/vdr/plugins/mcp/...

   Start with an ro key if the assistant should only look (see below).

3. Copy the certificate to the client and let the client trust it. Claude
   Code runs on Node.js and takes extra certificates from
   NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS:

     scp <vdr-host>:/var/lib/vdr/plugins/mcp/cert.pem ~/.config/vdr-mcp.pem
     export NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS=~/.config/vdr-mcp.pem   (e.g. in ~/.profile)

   On Windows:

     setx NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS "C:\Users\<you>\vdr-mcp.pem"

   then restart the client completely (for the Claude Code extension:
   restart VS Code), so it sees the variable.

4. Run the command line from step 2 on the client and start a new session.
   /mcp in Claude Code shows the server as connected.

The tools
---------

Which tools a client sees depends on the loaded plugins, the deny list and,
over HTTPS, the role of its key. The assistant reads the help page of each
tool and passes the SVDRP arguments; channels, timers and recordings are
addressed by the numbers the LST* commands return.

Tools beyond SVDRP:

  vdr_log       the newest lines of the system log of VDR, optionally
                filtered (only with --log, see below)
  vdr_marks     editing marks of a recording, its length before and after
                cutting
  vdr_setmarks  replaces all editing marks (h:mm:ss[.ff]), aligned to I-frames
  vdr_genindex  regenerates the index in the background like vdr --genindex
                and thereby counts the errors of the recording (line O of
                vdr_LSTR <id>, -1 = not checked)

These use VDR's plugin interface directly (cMarks, GenerateIndex(), the
recordings list), like other plugins do. vdr_setmarks and vdr_genindex
refuse recordings that are being recorded, replayed or cut. vdr_setmarks
needs the index of the recording; vdr_genindex works on one recording at a
time and only on TS recordings, not on old PES ones.

Not offered: QUIT, the deny list (default DEL*,HITK) and the plugin's own
commands (PLUG mcp ...), so no client can manage API keys.

Examples
--------

The assistant chooses the tools itself; the chains below show what happens
behind a request. Recording ids are those of vdr_LSTR; they stay the same
while VDR runs, but change with every restart of VDR.

Sort a series into seasons
  "Sort the recordings of Death in Paradise into seasons, named
   Season XX/YY. Title."
  vdr_LSTR lists the recordings, the assistant finds season and episode
  (from the description, or a web search if the client has one) and moves
  each recording, '~' separates the folders:
    vdr_MOVR 42 Death in Paradise~Season 03~05. Political Suicide

Find missing episodes and record them
  "Which episodes of Suits are missing, and when are they on again?"
  The assistant compares vdr_LSTR with an episode guide and the EPG
  (vdr_LSTE, epgsearch_QRYS) and sets timers with vdr_NEWT, e.g.
    vdr_NEWT 1:3:2026-11-10:1705:1800:50:99:Suits~Season 08~05. Title:

Timers before the EPG
  The EPG reaches 3 to 14 days ahead; broadcasters announce series weeks
  earlier. The assistant can set a fixed timer from such an announcement
  long before the EPG has the event, and later compare it with vdr_LSTE
  and correct it with vdr_MODT. epgsearch and VDR's pattern timers wait for
  the EPG; this is a third way. Watching the announcements needs a client
  that runs on a schedule, the plugin itself only answers.

Disable instead of delete
  With DEL* denied the assistant cannot delete a timer, but it can switch
  it off:
    vdr_MODT 6 off
  Deleting stays with the human, and nothing is lost by mistake.

Check recordings for errors
  "Which recordings have errors?"
  vdr_LSTR marks a recording with errors by '!' after its length,
  vdr_LSTR <id> shows the number in line O (-1 = not checked yet, e.g. a
  recording copied from elsewhere). vdr_genindex <id> checks it; vdr_log
  with filter "errors" shows when errors came in during a recording.

Cut out the advertising with markad
  vdr_LSTR <id> path        the directory of the recording
  markad_MARK <path>        markad sets the marks
  markad_STATUS             until it reports no running markad
  vdr_marks <id>            check the marks (and correct them with
                            vdr_setmarks if needed)
  vdr_EDIT <id>             cut; the cut recording gets a new index and
                            thereby an error count
  Note that the reply of markad_STATUS when it is done ("no active
  recording with running markad found") contains the word "running".

Status and reception
  "How full is the disk, which tuners are busy?"
  vdr_STAT disk, satip_INFO, femon or the like if loaded.

Security
--------

An rw client can do everything SVDRP can do, except what is denied: set and
change timers, rename and cut recordings, switch channels, start a replay.
Think of it as handing out the remote control and the recordings menu.

- The local server on 127.0.0.1:6420 has no authentication of its own;
  over SSH, SSH does the login. Do not use --bind with another address
  unless you know why: the port is then open to everyone in the network.
- Over HTTPS every request needs an API key. ro keys can only list and
  show. The plugin stores only the SHA-256 hash of a key.
- The deny list hides commands from every client, whatever its key.
  The default DEL*,HITK hides deleting (DELC, DELR, DELT and every plugin
  command starting with DEL) and the remote control keys. To hide more,
  e.g. cutting and moving:  --deny=DEL*,HITK,EDIT,MOVR
- Do not expose the HTTPS port to the internet; for access from outside
  use a VPN.

Deny list
---------

--deny takes a comma separated list. A trailing '*' matches any rest,
PLUGIN.CMD a single plugin command (e.g. epgsearch.UPDS), the tools beyond
SVDRP go by their name (e.g. vdr_setmarks). --deny= without a list offers
every command. The Debian package ships /etc/vdr/conf.avail/mcp.conf with
--deny=DEL*,HITK, so the file shows what is denied; without the option the
plugin uses the same list.

MCP over HTTPS
--------------

With --http=ADDR:PORT the plugin speaks MCP's "Streamable HTTP" transport at
https://<vdr-host>:PORT/mcp. The local server on 127.0.0.1:6420 stays as it
is.

Keys are managed over SVDRP on the VDR host:

  svdrpsend PLUG mcp KEYADD <name> ro|rw   creates a key, shown only once
  svdrpsend PLUG mcp KEYLIST               name, role, date
  svdrpsend PLUG mcp KEYDEL <name>         revokes it at once

The hashes are kept in /var/lib/vdr/plugins/mcp/keys. A name is 1-32
characters A-Z a-z 0-9 _ - . To replace a key, KEYDEL it and KEYADD it
again; KEYADD never overwrites an existing key. Then put the new key into
the client and restart the client: Claude Code keeps the header of a
server from the start of a session, a reconnect does not read it again.

ro keys may use HELP, LSTC, LSTD, LSTE, LSTR, LSTT, NEXT, PING, STAT,
vdr_log and vdr_marks. --ro-allow=LIST adds more, e.g.
--ro-allow=epgsearch.LSTS,epgsearch.QRYS,satip.INFO. rw keys may use every
command that is not denied.

TLS: the self-signed certificate (EC P-256, 10 years) is valid for the host
name, the full host name, localhost and the IPv4 addresses of the VDR host;
the log shows its SHA-256 fingerprint at every start. Delete cert.pem and
key.pem in /var/lib/vdr/plugins/mcp/ and restart VDR for a new one, e.g.
after a change of the host name or address; the clients then need the new
cert.pem. --cert=FILE and --key=FILE use your own certificate.
--http-plain switches TLS off, only for tests: the keys then travel
unencrypted.

Protocol details: each POST is answered with application/json (no
server-sent events), GET is refused with 405, DELETE ends a session.
A session belongs to the key that started it and ends after one hour
without requests. Requests with an Origin header are refused, as browsers
send one and MCP clients do not (protection against DNS rebinding).

System log
----------

With --log the server offers the tool vdr_log: the newest lines of the
systemd journal of VDR (unit vdr.service, or the one given as --log=UNIT),
optionally filtered by a text. It is off by default, because VDR runs as
the user vdr, which may not read the journal. To use it:

  adduser vdr systemd-journal

and restart VDR. Note that VDR can then read the whole system journal, not
only its own lines. The package does not change any group by itself.

Troubleshooting
---------------

The client shows the server as failed (SSH)
  The VDR host is off or asleep, or ssh asks for a password. Test
  "ssh <user>@<vdr-host> true" by hand, then reconnect in the client.

401, "missing or wrong API key"
  The key in the client is not (or no longer) on the VDR host, see
  KEYLIST and the log ("request from ... without a valid API key").
  After a KEYDEL/KEYADD the client must be restarted. To compare without
  showing the key: printf '%s' '<key>' | sha256sum gives the hash that
  must be in /var/lib/vdr/plugins/mcp/keys.

Certificate errors ("self-signed certificate", "unable to verify")
  The client does not trust cert.pem: NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS not set, or set
  after the client was started, or pointing to an old certificate. The
  name in the URL must be one the certificate was made for, see
  "openssl x509 -in cert.pem -noout -ext subjectAltName".

403 Forbidden
  The request carried an Origin header, i.e. it came from a browser.

A tool is missing
  Denied, not allowed for an ro key, or its plugin is not loaded. The tool
  list is built when a client connects; after loading a plugin, reconnect.

vdr_log is empty
  The user vdr is not in the group systemd-journal, or VDR was not
  restarted after adduser.

A recording id does not fit
  VDR was restarted in between; list the recordings again with vdr_LSTR.

Debian packages
---------------

The Debian packaging is kept in the branch debian/trixie of this
repository, on top of the release tag. To build the package yourself:

  git clone https://github.com/unf/vdr-plugin-mcp.git
  cd vdr-plugin-mcp
  git checkout debian/trixie
  gbp buildpackage

This needs git-buildpackage and vdr-dev of the VDR the plugin is built for.

Options
-------

  -b ADDR,   --bind=ADDR        address of the local MCP server (default:
                                127.0.0.1), other addresses are reachable
                                WITHOUT authentication
  -p PORT,   --port=PORT        TCP port of the local MCP server
                                (default: 6420)
  -s PORT,   --svdrp-port=PORT  SVDRP port of this VDR (default: 6419)
  -d LIST,   --deny=LIST        SVDRP commands that are not offered
                                (default: DEL*,HITK)
  -m BYTES,  --max-reply=BYTES  cut longer SVDRP replies (default: 262144)
  -l,        --log[=UNIT]       offer the tool vdr_log with the journal of the
                                systemd unit UNIT (default: vdr.service)
  -w ADDR:PORT, --http=ADDR:PORT  MCP over HTTPS with API keys (default: off)
  -c FILE,   --cert=FILE        own TLS certificate
  -k FILE,   --key=FILE         its private key
  -n,        --http-plain       HTTP without TLS, only for tests
  -r LIST,   --ro-allow=LIST    more commands for read only keys
